    "title": "SpaceX launch recap: Live updates from Starlink mission from Cape Canaveral",
    "text": "8:08 am ET April 2, 2026Brevard County deactivates launch support after successful SpaceX liftoffRick NealeBrevard County Emergency Management officials have deactivated the agency's launch operations support team after SpaceX’s successful Falcon 9 launch.8:03 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX Falcon 9 booster lands on drone ship in Atlantic OceanRick NealeThe Falcon 9 first-stage booster just landed aboard SpaceX's drone ship A Shortfall of Gravitas in the Atlantic Ocean, completing its 15th mission.7:55 am ET April 2, 2026Liftoff!Rick NealeLiftoff! SpaceX has just launched the Falcon 9 carrying 29 Starlink satellites from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.7:49 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX rocket launch webcast beginsRick NealeSpaceX's launch webcast is now posted above, right below our countdown clock.Liftoff is scheduled in six minutes from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.7:45 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX rocket launch coming up from Cape Canaveral, FloridaRick NealeTen minutes before SpaceX's Falcon 9 lifts off, the countdown appears to be proceeding as planned.Following is a list of key remaining countdown milestones. T-minus:7 minutes: Falcon 9 begins engine chill prior to launch.1 minute: Command flight computer begins final prelaunch checks; propellant tank pressurization to flight pressure begins.45 seconds: SpaceX launch director verifies “go for launch.”3 seconds: Engine controller commands engine ignition sequence to start.0 seconds: Falcon 9 liftoff.7:36 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX rocket booster to land on drone ship Just Read the InstructionsRick NealeToday's morning mission will mark the Falcon 9 first-stage booster’s 15th flight, SpaceX reported.This booster previously launched Crew-9, RRT-1, Firefly Blue Ghost Mission 1, Fram2, SXM-10, MTG-S1, EchoStar XXV and seven Starlink missions.Following stage separation, crews expect the booster to land on the SpaceX drone ship Just Read the Instructions in the Atlantic Ocean 8 minutes and 19 seconds after liftoff.7:26 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket fueling likely underway at Cape CanaveralRick NealeFalcon 9 fueling procedures are now likely taking place at Launch Complex 40, as evidenced by SpaceX's continuing countdown.That means the Starlink mission is locked in to lift off at 7:55 a.m. without any delays, or else today's launch must be postponed to a later date.7:13 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX launch to be 24th of year from Florida’s Space CoastRick NealeAfter Wednesday's historic NASA Artemis II liftoff, SpaceX's Falcon 9 launch will clock in as the 24th orbital rocket launch thus far during 2026 from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station and NASA's Kennedy Space Center.Click here for the FLORIDA TODAY Space Team's updating list detailing all of this year’s missions, complete with photo galleries and story links.7:03 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX launch prep underway in Brevard CountyRick NealeBrevard County Emergency Management officials have activated the agency's launch operations support team ahead of SpaceX’s upcoming Falcon 9 launch.6:52 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket launch countdown highlightsRick NealeHere's a step-by-step guide to the minutes leading up to launch from SpaceX’s behind-the-scenes Falcon 9 countdown. T-minus:38 minutes: SpaceX launch director verifies “go” for propellant load.35 minutes: Rocket-grade kerosene and first-stage liquid oxygen loading begins.16 minutes: Second-stage liquid oxygen loading begins.7 minutes: Falcon 9 begins engine chill prior to launch.1 minute: Command flight computer begins final prelaunch checks; propellant tank pressurization to flight pressure begins.45 seconds: SpaceX launch director verifies “go” for launch.3 seconds: Engine controller commands engine ignition sequence to start.0 seconds: Liftoff.6:41 am ET April 2, 2026SpaceX launch forecast details from Space Force meteorologistsRick NealeFrom the 45th Weather Squadron's forecast discussion, which focuses on an easterly wave embedded within the Space Coast's prolonged onshore flow:\"This will enhance rain shower development, especially in the late morning when combined withdaytime heating and localized sea breeze effects,\" the narrative said.\"The Cumulus Cloud Rule will be a growing concern throughout (today's) launch window, along with the Surface Electric Fields Rule if showers gain intensity,\" the narrative said.6:21 am ET April 2, 2026Fair skies reported at Cape Canaveral ahead of SpaceX Starlink launchRick NealeAs of 5:55 a.m., the National Weather Service reported fair skies, a temperature of 70 degrees, east wind of 8 mph, and 10-mile visibility at the Space Force installation.",
